<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Epane, Epona Augusta, Epona Regina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epona was the goddess of horses and ponies in Gallo-Roman religion, and also a fertility and mother goddess. Her name, from Gaulish, means 'Great Mare'. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a> she watched over horses, donkeys, mules and their riders, and she was often shown riding sidesaddle or seated between horses or foals, holding a cornucopia, fruit or grain. Cavalry units recruited from Gaul, Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> spread her cult across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, where she was widely worshipped as patroness of cavalry between the 1st and 3rd centuries CE. She was the only Celtic divinity ultimately worshipped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> itself, invoked for the emperor as Epona Augusta or Epona Regina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epona.jpg" alt="Epona" width="330" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rosemania · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cernunnos, the horned god, and <b>Epona</b>, goddess of horses, were widely known, and triplicity was common, as in the Three Mothers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions record hundreds of divine names, many of them local: Lugus, remembered in the name of Lugdunum, modern Lyon; <b>Epona</b>, the horse goddess, popular in the Roman army; Cernunnos, named on a carved pillar from Paris; and Sulis, goddess of the hot springs at Bath.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped many gods, among them the thunder god Taranis, Lugus, a god of skills and crafts, and the horse goddess <b>Epona</b>, held ceremonies in sacred groves and offered treasured things in rivers, wetlands and shafts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
